Z||000007||Fleabitdog||07-13-2001||10:09 AM||||Fierojunky<BR>   I was not cited because of the degree of tint. The ticket was for aftermarket tint. But that was about 6 years ago and I don't know if they have changed the laws since. And not long after that I was pulled in New Jersey for tinted windows. The cop told me they were illegal there but just gave me a warning since I was out of state. It is rediculous. You see limos and suv's that have so much tint you cannot see in the car but yet I get pulled for aftermarket tint that was a legal degree of tint for Pa.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||07-13-2001||12:01 PM||||Ok here we go, the law is as stated below for PA<P>The % is the amount of light that must pass through the window.<P>Pre 1998 Passenger Cars<BR>Windshield 70%<BR>Front side 70%<BR>Rear side 70%<BR>Rear      VESC-20 complient<P>1998 or newer<BR>Pre 1998 Passenger Cars<BR>Windshield 70%<BR>Front side 70%<BR>Rear side 70%<BR>Rear      70%<P>Trucks and multi purpose passenger vehicles,<BR>Medium/Heavy trucks and buses,<BR>and all other vehicles,<BR>Windshield 70%<BR>Front side 70%<BR>Rear side No requirement<BR>Rear      No requirement<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000009||fierojunky||07-13-2001||12:50 PM||||84Bill - please cite your source.  PA Law is as follows according to Title 75 of the PA Code Section 4524 Windshield obstructions and wipers Subsection (e) Sun screening and other materials prohibited.  (1) No person shall drive any motor vehicle with any sun screening device or other material which does not permit a peson t osee or view the inside of the vehicle through the windshield, side wing or side window of the vehicle.  (2) This subsection does not apply to:  (i) a vehicle which is equipped iwth tinted windows of the type and specification that were installed by the manufacturer of the vehicle or to any hearse, ambulance, government vehicle or any other vehicle for which a currently valid certificate of exemption has been issued in accordance with regulations adopted by the department.  (ii) A vehicle which is equipped with tinted windows, sun screening devices or other materials which comply with all applicable Federal regulations and for which a currently valid certificate of exemption for medical reasons has been issued in accordance with regulations adopted by the department.  (3) A certificate of exemption shall be issued by the department for a vehicle which is:  (i) Registerd in this Commonwealth on the effective date of this subsection and is equipped with a sun screening device or other material prohibited under paragraph (1) on the effective date.  (ii) Equipoped with tinted windows, sun screening devices or other materials for a physical condition that makes it necessary to equip the motor vehicle with sun screening material which would be of a light transmittance or luminous reflectance in violation of this section.  (A) A certificate of exemption for medical reason shall be issued only if the owner or registrant of the vehicle, or a person residing in the household of the owner or registrant who regularly drives or is driven in the vehicle, suffers from a physical condition determined by the department, in consultation with the Medical Advisory Board, to justify the exemption... ETC.  I don't want to take up the forum with this.  As you see there are NO %'s in the Pennsylvania code.  The windows in my truck are tinted 5% and i have argued this all 3 times i got cited and NEVER lost. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||07-13-2001||02:36 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.dot4.state.pa.us/fact_sheets/index.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dot4.state.pa.us/fact_sheets/index.shtml</A> <P>Follow this link it will list among other things "Vehicle Sun Screening" and the qualifications for "Antique, Classic and Collectable Vehicles"<P>Almost everything you wanted to know but were afraid to ask.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000011||fierojunky||07-13-2001||02:44 PM||||Again - that web site doesn't site an authority  - They just say fact sheets.  What i posted before is the actual PA Code.  What the web page says might be for vehicle inspection stations.  Like i said every time i get a ticket for my tint i take a copy of the PA Code along with any of the current suppliments and haven't paid a ticket or removed my tint yet.  I'm not trying to flame you Bill.  I'm just saying unless someone provides me with a higher authority different from what the PA Code says.  My tint will stay.||||reg||1
